            "name": "drainage",
            "statement": "C-0004's §4(d) discharge",
            "verdict": "HOLDS — the arms stand on the side of the tile AWAY from the layer, so the squeeze film is untouched and what they add is 9.1 % of the total drag as an UPPER bound; the worst 40 x 40 nm margin falls from 22.81x to 20.73x"
        }
    ],
    "findings": [
        "A body attached to a structure at ONE point and otherwise free adds EXACTLY ZERO static stiffness there. Its rigid-body motions span everything a single crossover can impose — the deflection through the vertical link and the roll through k_theta — so the minimised arm energy is identically zero and the Schur complement vanishes term by term. 34 arms covering 46 % of the tile's plan change nothing in C-0009's grillage, and that is a symmetry statement rather than a small number.",
        "The single tie is NOT a modelling choice. An upward site's lattice pitch is the bare 32 bp = 10.88 nm because it belongs to one duplex, and C-0039's elastica gives 8.164 nm at C-0055's self-consistent 34 paths and 9.131 nm even at §3's 45 — so no arm in the design range can reach a second root. The escape, its price and now its structural harmlessness are all the SAME sentence.",
        "What the exact zero is a zero OF, in three steps rather than two. ONE tie adds nothing at all. TWO ties add a TORSION BAR and nothing else — 5.832 pN nm/rad, the two hinges in series with the arm's own GJ/L — and no bending stiffness whatever, because two points determine a line and a rigid arm can meet both: the peak dishing moves 0.112 %. The arm's own EI is engaged only at THREE ties, i.e. 21.76 nm of arm, and a genuinely axially coupled second layer would carry 19.3x one duplex's own EI. The whole bracket is decided by inequalities between lengths.",
        "The cheap bound decided the order of work and it was right: rigidity EXACTLY zero, inertia 1.0e-12 of the actuation load at 1 kHz, drag 9.1 % of C-0004's budget. Only the third channel moves a number, and it moves the one quantity in this programme that is quoted with a bandwidth.",
        "The arms do not enter the actuation gap at all — they stand on the +z face, the PEG layer and the electrode are on the -z face — so no electrostatic, osmotic or squeeze-film quantity in this programme has an arm term in it. What they enter is the space ABOVE, which is C-0035's output superstructure, and that is a clearance question this claim states rather than solves.",
        "The coupling entering at the 34 arm roots rather than on C-0015's 3 x 15 grid is a real change and it is the only one: 0.4156 of the stroke against 0.2182, both past T-5b's 0.10, and 1.35x the FREE tile's own 0.3079. The arm roots are a 10.88 nm pitch along x on alternating rows, so the array is a coarser and less regular grid than the one C-0047 and C-0058 optimise over — and on it a uniform coupling is a net dishing SOURCE, which is the pathology C-0058 found at C-0041's 1 x 15.",
        "C-0055's placement is not centro-symmetric and nothing upstream noticed: its scheduler fills every row from the low-x end and points every arm the same way, so the coupling centroid sits at x = -8.80 nm on a tile that runs -20 to +20. Reflecting the odd rows — free, on the same column lattice, and inside C-0055's own per-row independence — moves the centroid to 2.08 nm and the dishing from 0.4156 to 0.3558 of the stroke.",
        "C-0058's non-uniform axis was written on C-0015's 3 x 15 grid and it does NOT transfer to the stations the buildable array supplies. Its rim x 5 rule reaches 0.0753 there (reproducing its published 0.0753) and only 0.3419 on C-0055's own roots and 0.2250 on the mirrored ones; the best ratio swept reaches 0.1649. A distribution cannot repair a placement — C-0058's own sentence, at a placement it did not test."
    ],
